I can confirm the same issue on tailscaled v1.76.1 from Nix on macOS 15.1. For me, manually creating a file of my domain under `/etc/resolver/` fixes the issue. If you are using nix-darwin, then something like this helps:

```nix
      environment.etc."resolver/<redacted-host>" = {
        text = ''
          nameserver participant17.participant17.participant17.participant17
          search <redacted-host>
        '';
      };
```

@participant7 your message got me curious, so I tried turning on `--accept-dns=true`, then having macOS forget my network settings for one of the split band networks in my home, then connected to that network and didn't set anything manually in the system preferences for DNS, to see if perhaps something in my existing setup was getting in the way of the workaround you posted.

With that having been done, my system preferences app says that I've got the same DNS settings that I would expect from my DHCP server (has the router as the DNS resolver, and no search domains, which I believe the DHCP server doesn't set, even though it does provide a `.lan` domain and I have traditionally added that as a search domain to my machines.) `tailscale status` complains about not being able to talk to DNS, but I can still resolve names from both my tailnet and local network (note: I have split DNS and my local network's search domain set in the tailscale control panel online, but that was for the sake of my linux machines; I've been used to having to manually set up MagicDNS per the instructions that have been in the wiki all this time.) Even after flushing the DNS cache and HUPing the `mDNSResponder` processes as you originally wrote about, I can still resolve those names, yet `tailscale status` is still complaining. `dscacheutil -q host -a name <host>` works but `dns-sd -G v4 <host>` or `dns-sd -q <host>` print some stuff that indicates the record is resolvable, but then they hang indefinitely. Very strange. It's also worth noting that while `tailscale status` is saying tailscale can't reach the configured DNS servers, `tailscale dns status` isn't showing any warnings.

I don't know if it'll end up being like [my earlier "workaround" that just left me in a state where resolution was up to a coin flip](https://github.com/tailscale/tailscale/issues/13461#issuecomment-2357479403), I'll have to report back later about that. But I figured I'd mention this behavior now in case it helps anyone else in this thread.
